  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user: \"Write a detailed tutorial on implementing a bounded LRU cache in Python using collections.OrderedDict. Include a complete class, explain get and put behavior, discuss edge cases, and include tests. Continue with a worked example and complexity analysis. Be precise and use meaningful prose and code.\"\n\nWe need produce final answer with detailed tutorial. Need be precise. We can provide code. Need likely include Python class LRUCache using OrderedDict. Explain get/put. Edge cases: capacity <=0? Key existence? Values can be None? Updating existing key moves to end? Removing oldest? Type checking? Hashable keys? Python hash? OrderedDict.move_to_end(last=False) for most recently used? Need choose convention. Typically most recently used at end. get moves to end, put existing moves to end; if capacity exceeded popitem(last=False). Need capacity non-positive: could raise ValueError or return None? Need define. Include tests. Worked example with sequence operations and table. Complexity analysis O(1) average. Also mention OrderedDict maintains insertion order and move_to_end. Maybe include tests using unittest and doctest? Need complete.\n\nNeed think about potential subtlety: In Python collections.OrderedDict, move_to_end(key, last=True). popitem(last=False) removes first (least recently used if MRU at end). For put: if key in self.cache: self.cache[key]=value; self.cache.move_to_end(key); else: if len(cache) >= capacity: self.cache.popitem(last=False); self.cache[key]=value. Need if capacity 0? If allow capacity 0, any put should evict? But if capacity is 0, len(cache) >= 0 before insertion would popitem on empty causing KeyError. So need handle. Options: require capacity > 0, else raise ValueError. Or if capacity <=0, get returns None, put no-op. But bounded LRU usually capacity positive. For robust, in __init__ if capacity < 0 raise ValueError? Or if capacity <=0 set to 0? Need edge cases. Let's define: capacity must be non-negative integer; if negative raise ValueError; if zero, cache stores nothing, get returns None, put is no-op. But typical Leetcode capacity positive. Maybe better: require positive integer: raise ValueError if capacity <=0. But edge cases could include capacity zero. If we require positive, simpler. But \"edge cases\" maybe capacity <=0. Could implement support for zero by no-op.",
